---
title: Non-Autonomous Linear ODE (x' = A(t)x) Scaling Benchmarks
author: Arnav Kapoor
---

# How does solving x' = A(t)x scale with system size?

This benchmark addresses [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#404](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404):
for a general non-autonomous linear system `x'(t) = A(t) x(t)`, (i) how does wall-clock
time and memory to solve it scale with the system size `N`, and (ii) how large an `N x N`
system (with a non-trivial number of time steps, up to millions) can be solved on a single
machine? As
[noted in the issue thread](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404#issuecomment-1019531233),
`x' = A(t)x` is structurally different from a generic ODE: specialized **Magnus and
matrix-exponential (Lie group) integrators** exist specifically for this class of problem,
and this page benchmarks them against a general-purpose explicit solver. Per that same
comment, this is deliberately scoped to a single machine (no multi-node/MPI), and the test
problem is built exactly as suggested there: a method-of-lines discretization of a diffusion
PDE, so this also illustrates how solve cost scales as a PDE is discretized more finely in
space.

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEqLinear, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5
using SciMLOperators, LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays
using BenchmarkTools, Plots, Printf
gr(fmt = :png)
```

## The model: a diffusion equation with time-varying diffusivity

`du/dt = kappa(t) * u_xx` on `[0, 1]` with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions and
`kappa(t) = 1 + 0.5*sin(t)` (bounded, strictly positive, so the problem stays well-posed for
all `t`). Discretizing `u_xx` with a standard second-order central difference on `N`
interior grid points gives exactly a linear, non-autonomous ODE system `x'(t) = A(t) x(t)`,
with `A(t) = kappa(t) * L`, `L` the (fixed, sparse, tridiagonal) discrete Laplacian scaled
by `1/dx^2`. This is precisely the "continually discretize the PDE in space" scaling
question raised in the issue.

```julia
function laplacian_operator(N)
    dx = 1.0 / (N + 1)
    L = (1 / dx^2) .* spdiagm(-1 => fill(1.0, N - 1), 0 => fill(-2.0, N), 1 => fill(1.0, N - 1))
    return L, dx
end

function linear_problem(N; tspan = (0.0, 0.1))
    L, dx = laplacian_operator(N)
    update_func!(A, u, p, t) = (A .= (1 + 0.5 * sin(t)) .* L)
    A = MatrixOperator(copy(L); update_func! = update_func!)
    u0 = [sin(pi * i * dx) for i in 1:N]
    return ODEProblem(A, u0, tspan), L, u0
end

function reference_problem(N; tspan = (0.0, 0.1))
    L, dx = laplacian_operator(N)
    function f!(du, u, p, t)
        mul!(du, L, u)
        du .*= (1 + 0.5 * sin(t))
    end
    u0 = [sin(pi * i * dx) for i in 1:N]
    return ODEProblem(f!, u0, tspan)
end
```

`linear_problem` wraps `A(t)` as a `SciMLOperators.MatrixOperator` with an `update_func!`,
the representation the specialized linear/Magnus solvers require to exploit the problem's
linearity; `reference_problem` is the exact same dynamics written as an ordinary
(nonlinear-looking) `ODEFunction`, solvable by any general-purpose method for comparison.

## Correctness check

```julia
N_check = 200
probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N_check)
probF = reference_problem(N_check)

sol_exp = solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
sol_tsit5 = solve(probF, Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-10, abstol = 1e-10)

println("max abs difference: ", maximum(abs.(sol_exp.u[end] .- sol_tsit5.u[end])))
```

The two solutions agree to within the truncation error implied by `LinearExponential`'s
fixed `dt = 0.001` (it must resolve `kappa(t)`'s variation using its own step size, unlike
`Tsit5`'s adaptive, tightly-toleranced stepping), which is more than sufficient for the
scaling comparisons below.

## Scaling with system size: general-purpose vs. specialized solvers

`Tsit5` is a high-order explicit Runge-Kutta method with no knowledge of the problem's
linear structure; `LinearExponential` computes each step as a (here, Krylov-subspace)
matrix-exponential action `exp(A(t) dt) u`, exploiting linearity directly and avoiding the
explicit stability restriction entirely. As `N` grows, the discrete Laplacian's eigenvalues
spread over a range that grows like `N^2` (finer grid spacing means stiffer dynamics), which
forces an explicit method's *stable* step size down like `1/N^2` -- so its total step count,
and hence wall-clock time, should grow roughly like `N^2`, while the exponential
integrator's step count is set by *accuracy*, not stability, and stays fixed as `N` grows.

```julia
Ns_compare = [50, 100, 200, 400, 800]
times_exp = Float64[]
times_tsit5 = Float64[]
mem_exp = Float64[]
mem_tsit5 = Float64[]
steps_tsit5 = Int[]

# warm up (compilation)
let (p, _, _) = linear_problem(10)
    solve(p, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
end
solve(reference_problem(10), Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-6, abstol = 1e-6)

for N in Ns_compare
    local probA, probF
    probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N)
    probF = reference_problem(N)

    t1 = @belapsed solve($probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001) samples=3 evals=1
    b1 = @ballocated solve($probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001) samples=1 evals=1
    t2 = @belapsed solve($probF, Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-6, abstol = 1e-6) samples=3 evals=1
    b2 = @ballocated solve($probF, Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-6, abstol = 1e-6) samples=1 evals=1

    push!(times_exp, t1); push!(mem_exp, b1 / 1e6)
    push!(times_tsit5, t2); push!(mem_tsit5, b2 / 1e6)
    push!(steps_tsit5, length(solve(probF, Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-6, abstol = 1e-6).t))

    @printf("N=%-5d  LinearExponential: %8.4fs %8.2fMB   Tsit5: %8.4fs %8.2fMB (%d steps)\n",
        N, t1, b1 / 1e6, t2, b2 / 1e6, steps_tsit5[end])
end
```

```julia
plot(Ns_compare, times_exp, label = "LinearExponential", marker = :circle,
    xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10, xlabel = "N", ylabel = "wall-clock time (s)",
    title = "Time to solve vs. system size N", legend = :topleft, size = (700, 450))
plot!(Ns_compare, times_tsit5, label = "Tsit5", marker = :square)
```

```julia
plot(Ns_compare, mem_exp, label = "LinearExponential", marker = :circle,
    xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10, xlabel = "N", ylabel = "memory allocated (MB)",
    title = "Memory vs. system size N", legend = :topleft, size = (700, 450))
plot!(Ns_compare, mem_tsit5, label = "Tsit5", marker = :square)
```

Tsit5's step count (and hence its cost) grows dramatically faster than `N` as the problem
stiffens, exactly as the `1/N^2` stability-restricted step size argument predicts;
`LinearExponential`'s cost grows far more mildly, since it takes the same fixed number of
steps regardless of `N` (only each step's own sparse-matrix-exponential-action cost grows
with `N`).

## Pushing system size as far as practical on a single machine

With `Tsit5` no longer competitive at this scale, we push `LinearExponential` alone to much
larger `N`, directly answering "how big an `N x N` system can you solve" on one machine in a
non-trivial amount of wall-clock time.

```julia
Ns_push = [1_000, 5_000, 10_000, 25_000, 50_000]
times_push = Float64[]
mem_push = Float64[]

for N in Ns_push
    local probA
    probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N)
    t = @elapsed sol = solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
    b = @allocated solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
    push!(times_push, t); push!(mem_push, b / 1e6)
    @printf("N=%-6d  time=%8.3fs   memory=%8.2fMB\n", N, t, b / 1e6)
end
```

```julia
plot(Ns_push, times_push, marker = :circle, xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "N", ylabel = "wall-clock time (s)",
    title = "LinearExponential: pushing system size", legend = false, size = (700, 450))
```

## Scaling with the number of time steps

The issue also asks about pushing the number of time steps into the thousands-to-millions
range. Since each `LinearExponential` step is an independent Krylov exponential-action
(rather than something whose cost depends on how many steps came before it), total cost is
expected to grow roughly in proportion to the number of steps taken, up to run-to-run
variance in exactly how many Krylov iterations a given step needs to converge.

```julia
N_fixed = 200
probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N_fixed)
n_steps_list = [10^3, 10^4, 10^5, 10^6]
times_steps = Float64[]

for n_steps in n_steps_list
    dt = 0.1 / n_steps
    t = @elapsed solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = dt)
    push!(times_steps, t)
    @printf("n_steps=%-9d  time=%8.3fs   (%.2e s/step)\n", n_steps, t, t / n_steps)
end
```

```julia
plot(n_steps_list, times_steps, marker = :circle, xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "number of time steps", ylabel = "wall-clock time (s)",
    title = "LinearExponential: scaling with step count (N=$N_fixed)",
    legend = false, size = (700, 450))
```

# Conclusion

For a non-autonomous linear system `x' = A(t)x` built from a method-of-lines PDE
discretization, a general-purpose explicit integrator (`Tsit5`) and a specialized
matrix-exponential integrator (`LinearExponential`, from the Magnus/Lie-group family in
`OrdinaryDiffEqLinear`) agree on the solution, but scale completely differently as the
system grows: the explicit method's cost explodes once the discretization's implied
stiffness (`~N^2` for a diffusion operator) restricts its stable step size, while the
exponential integrator's cost grows far more gently, since it isn't stability-restricted at
all. That gap lets a single machine push this class of problem to system sizes and step
counts (tens of thousands of unknowns, up to a million time steps, in this benchmark) that
would be entirely impractical for a general-purpose solver at the same accuracy target.
Per the [issue discussion](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404), this is
intentionally single-node in scope; extending it to distributed/HPC-scale problems (and to
other PDE benchmarks such as Brusselator or Gray-Scott, as suggested there) is a natural,
larger follow-up.

```julia, echo = false
using SciMLBenchmarks
SciMLBenchmarks.bench_footer(WEAVE_ARGS[:folder], WEAVE_ARGS[:file])
```
